1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,474
Sweetheart, in December of two thousand nine,

2
00:00:03,474 --> 00:00:06,562
a young clerk at the fanciest department

3
00:00:06,562 --> 00:00:09,187
store in Columbus looked at my old

4
00:00:09,187 --> 00:00:10,963
coat and my snow boots,

5
00:00:10,963 --> 00:00:12,893
and she told me to leave,

6
00:00:12,893 --> 00:00:15,673
because the store was for customers.

7
00:00:15,853 --> 00:00:18,111
What she didn't know was that I

8
00:00:18,111 --> 00:00:20,078
had a check in my purse for

9
00:00:20,078 --> 00:00:21,607
ten thousand dollars,

10
00:00:21,607 --> 00:00:24,011
and I'd come to spend every penny

11
00:00:24,011 --> 00:00:25,177
of it, that day,

12
00:00:25,177 --> 00:00:26,488
in her department.

13
00:00:26,838 --> 00:00:29,199
Tonight I'm going to tell you where

14
00:00:29,199 --> 00:00:30,616
that check came from,

15
00:00:30,616 --> 00:00:32,370
what I said to that clerk,

16
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:35,069
and what happened when the store manager

17
00:00:35,069 --> 00:00:37,228
came running down the escalator.

18
00:00:37,408 --> 00:00:38,407
Stay with me,

19
00:00:38,407 --> 00:00:41,866
honey, because the ending isn't about getting

20
00:00:41,866 --> 00:00:42,250
even.

21
00:00:42,430 --> 00:00:44,844
It's about what that young woman did

22
00:00:44,844 --> 00:00:46,051
three weeks later,

23
00:00:46,051 --> 00:00:47,727
and it made me cry in the

24
00:00:47,727 --> 00:00:49,001
middle of a church.

25
00:00:49,351 --> 00:00:51,309
Every winter at our church,

26
00:00:51,309 --> 00:00:53,268
we had a coat drive for the

27
00:00:53,268 --> 00:00:55,516
children at Lincoln Elementary,

28
00:00:55,516 --> 00:00:57,837
the poorest school in Mansfield.

29
00:00:58,017 --> 00:01:00,720
People would bring in their old coats,

30
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,995
and we'd wash them and mend them

31
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:04,275
and hand them out.

32
00:01:04,455 --> 00:01:05,443
But that year,

33
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:07,773
the coats coming in were thin and

34
00:01:07,773 --> 00:01:08,902
worn out, honey.

35
00:01:09,082 --> 00:01:10,767
And I saw a little boy at

36
00:01:10,767 --> 00:01:13,060
the bus stop in December wearing a

37
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,431
summer windbreaker and two sweatshirts underneath.

38
00:01:16,781 --> 00:01:18,986
I went home and couldn't sleep.

39
00:01:19,166 --> 00:01:21,378
So I went to the church council

40
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:22,805
and said, this year,

41
00:01:22,805 --> 00:01:24,588
let's buy them new coats.

42
00:01:24,768 --> 00:01:26,612
Real winter coats,

43
00:01:26,612 --> 00:01:28,762
brand new, with tags.

44
00:01:28,942 --> 00:01:31,682
Every child at Lincoln who needs one.

45
00:01:31,862 --> 00:01:33,360
The council laughed.

46
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:34,759
They said, Ruth,

47
00:01:34,759 --> 00:01:37,656
there are a hundred and forty children

48
00:01:37,656 --> 00:01:38,799
at that school.

49
00:01:38,979 --> 00:01:40,863
Where are we going to get that

50
00:01:40,863 --> 00:01:41,742
kind of money?

51
00:01:41,922 --> 00:01:43,058
And I said,

52
00:01:43,058 --> 00:01:44,918
give me six weeks.

53
00:01:45,268 --> 00:01:46,590
For six weeks,

54
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:47,346
I baked.

55
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:49,267
Every single day.

56
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:52,253
Pies, cookies, cinnamon rolls,

57
00:01:52,253 --> 00:01:54,498
my mother's fruit bread.

58
00:01:54,678 --> 00:01:56,733
I sold them after church,

59
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:58,543
at the hardware store,

60
00:01:58,543 --> 00:01:59,859
at the hospital,

61
00:01:59,859 --> 00:02:01,585
at the bowling alley.

62
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:04,472
The ladies from my Bible study baked

63
00:02:04,472 --> 00:02:04,772
too.

64
00:02:04,952 --> 00:02:07,560
Then the newspaper wrote a little story

65
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:10,235
about it, and the checks started coming.

66
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:12,642
In a minute I'll tell you how

67
00:02:12,642 --> 00:02:13,794
much we raised.

68
00:02:13,974 --> 00:02:15,088
We were shocked.

69
00:02:15,438 --> 00:02:18,032
Ten thousand two hundred dollars,

70
00:02:18,032 --> 00:02:18,897
sweetheart.

71
00:02:19,077 --> 00:02:21,572
From pies and cookies and a little

72
00:02:21,572 --> 00:02:22,967
story in the paper.

73
00:02:23,147 --> 00:02:25,510
Enough for a hundred and forty good

74
00:02:25,510 --> 00:02:27,942
winter coats, with hats and mittens.

75
00:02:28,122 --> 00:02:30,473
The church treasurer wrote me a check,

76
00:02:30,473 --> 00:02:32,701
and I called the department store in

77
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:35,423
Columbus that had the best children's coats,

78
00:02:35,423 --> 00:02:37,466
and they said to come on Saturday

79
00:02:37,466 --> 00:02:40,312
and their children's department would help me.

80
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:42,260
Now, on that Saturday,

81
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:44,367
I'd been up since four in the

82
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:47,128
morning baking the last order of pies.

83
00:02:47,308 --> 00:02:48,531
I was tired,

84
00:02:48,531 --> 00:02:49,143
honey.

85
00:02:49,323 --> 00:02:51,353
I didn't think about what I was

86
00:02:51,353 --> 00:02:51,877
wearing.

87
00:02:52,057 --> 00:02:54,134
My old brown car coat from the

88
00:02:54,134 --> 00:02:56,973
nineteen eighties, the one with the patch

89
00:02:56,973 --> 00:02:57,873
on the elbow.

90
00:02:58,053 --> 00:03:00,910
My snow boots with the salt stains.

91
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:03,730
My hair under a plastic rain bonnet,

92
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:05,490
because it was sleeting.

93
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:07,876
I looked like what I was.

94
00:03:08,056 --> 00:03:10,819
A tired grandmother from Mansfield.

95
00:03:11,169 --> 00:03:13,963
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

96
00:03:13,963 --> 00:03:16,119
tap the like button for me,

97
00:03:16,119 --> 00:03:16,997
sweetheart.

98
00:03:17,177 --> 00:03:20,323
It helps more people find these stories.

99
00:03:20,503 --> 00:03:23,368
The children's department was on the third

100
00:03:23,368 --> 00:03:23,777
floor.

101
00:03:23,957 --> 00:03:25,292
It was beautiful.

102
00:03:25,472 --> 00:03:27,504
Christmas trees, music,

103
00:03:27,504 --> 00:03:31,126
little velvet coats on little mannequins.

104
00:03:31,306 --> 00:03:34,076
And behind the counter was a young

105
00:03:34,076 --> 00:03:36,031
woman, maybe twenty-two,

106
00:03:36,031 --> 00:03:38,720
very pretty, with long red nails,

107
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,838
talking on her cell phone.

108
00:03:41,188 --> 00:03:42,128
I waited.

109
00:03:42,308 --> 00:03:43,585
And waited.

110
00:03:43,765 --> 00:03:45,170
Five minutes.

111
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:45,988
Ten.

112
00:03:46,168 --> 00:03:47,399
She kept talking.

113
00:03:47,579 --> 00:03:49,227
Other customers came,

114
00:03:49,227 --> 00:03:51,817
in nice coats with shopping bags,

115
00:03:51,817 --> 00:03:54,251
and she hung up and helped them

116
00:03:54,251 --> 00:03:55,114
right away.

117
00:03:55,294 --> 00:03:57,651
Then she went back to her phone.

118
00:03:57,831 --> 00:03:59,244
Finally I said,

119
00:03:59,244 --> 00:04:00,752
excuse me, miss,

120
00:04:00,752 --> 00:04:04,332
I'm here to buy some children's coats.

121
00:04:04,512 --> 00:04:06,157
And she looked at me,

122
00:04:06,157 --> 00:04:07,097
up and down,

123
00:04:07,097 --> 00:04:10,231
from the rain bonnet to the salt-stained

124
00:04:10,231 --> 00:04:11,954
boots, and she sighed.

125
00:04:12,305 --> 00:04:13,436
She said, ma'am,

126
00:04:13,436 --> 00:04:16,124
the clearance rack is in the basement.

127
00:04:16,304 --> 00:04:18,905
These coats start at sixty dollars.

128
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:21,227
I said, I know,

129
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:21,941
dear.

130
00:04:22,121 --> 00:04:23,879
I'd like to buy a lot of

131
00:04:23,879 --> 00:04:24,246
them.

132
00:04:24,426 --> 00:04:25,819
And she laughed.

133
00:04:25,999 --> 00:04:27,765
She actually laughed,

134
00:04:27,765 --> 00:04:29,111
and said, ma'am,

135
00:04:29,111 --> 00:04:30,794
we don't do layaway.

136
00:04:30,974 --> 00:04:33,486
And then she leaned over the counter

137
00:04:33,486 --> 00:04:35,300
and said, a little louder,

138
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,951
honestly, this floor is for customers.

139
00:04:38,131 --> 00:04:40,302
You're making people uncomfortable.

140
00:04:40,482 --> 00:04:42,212
Maybe you should go.

141
00:04:42,563 --> 00:04:44,268
Everybody heard her,

142
00:04:44,268 --> 00:04:44,780
honey.

143
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:47,301
A woman with a stroller stared at

144
00:04:47,301 --> 00:04:47,514
me.

145
00:04:47,694 --> 00:04:49,366
A man looked away.

146
00:04:49,546 --> 00:04:51,719
I felt my face get hot all

147
00:04:51,719 --> 00:04:53,308
the way to my ears.

148
00:04:53,488 --> 00:04:54,539
For one second,

149
00:04:54,539 --> 00:04:56,501
I was ten years old again on

150
00:04:56,501 --> 00:04:58,954
Elm Street, in a hand-me-down coat,

151
00:04:58,954 --> 00:05:01,266
and the rich girls were laughing.

152
00:05:01,446 --> 00:05:03,153
I almost left.

153
00:05:03,333 --> 00:05:06,134
I almost turned around and walked right

154
00:05:06,134 --> 00:05:06,421
out.

155
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:08,620
And then I thought about that little

156
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:09,910
boy in the windbreaker.

157
00:05:10,261 --> 00:05:12,594
And I'll tell you something,

158
00:05:12,594 --> 00:05:13,511
sweetheart.

159
00:05:13,691 --> 00:05:16,109
I've been on both sides of that

160
00:05:16,109 --> 00:05:16,733
counter.

161
00:05:16,913 --> 00:05:18,189
When I was sixteen,

162
00:05:18,189 --> 00:05:20,540
I worked at the Woolworth's on Main

163
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,622
Street, and I saw how the girls

164
00:05:22,622 --> 00:05:24,905
treated the farm women who came in

165
00:05:24,905 --> 00:05:27,054
with mud on their boots and cash

166
00:05:27,054 --> 00:05:28,128
in a coffee can.

167
00:05:28,308 --> 00:05:31,199
Those farm women always had the cash.

168
00:05:31,379 --> 00:05:33,599
The ladies in the fur coats were

169
00:05:33,599 --> 00:05:35,887
the ones who bought on credit and

170
00:05:35,887 --> 00:05:36,650
never paid.

171
00:05:37,001 --> 00:05:38,498
So I didn't leave.

172
00:05:38,678 --> 00:05:40,131
I opened my purse,

173
00:05:40,131 --> 00:05:42,715
very slowly, and I took out that

174
00:05:42,715 --> 00:05:44,975
check, and I laid it flat on

175
00:05:44,975 --> 00:05:46,428
her glass counter,

176
00:05:46,428 --> 00:05:49,011
right in front of her red nails.

177
00:05:49,191 --> 00:05:51,629
Ten thousand two hundred dollars,

178
00:05:51,629 --> 00:05:53,255
made out to the store.

179
00:05:53,435 --> 00:05:54,269
And I said,

180
00:05:54,269 --> 00:05:57,075
quietly, dear, I'd like a hundred and

181
00:05:57,075 --> 00:05:58,516
forty winter coats,

182
00:05:58,516 --> 00:06:01,550
in children's sizes four through twelve.

183
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,460
With hats and mittens.

184
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,753
And I'd like to pay today.

185
00:06:06,103 --> 00:06:08,393
She looked at that check for a

186
00:06:08,393 --> 00:06:09,156
long time.

187
00:06:09,336 --> 00:06:11,043
She read it twice.

188
00:06:11,223 --> 00:06:12,764
Her face went white,

189
00:06:12,764 --> 00:06:13,842
and then pink,

190
00:06:13,842 --> 00:06:15,460
and then white again.

191
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,916
She said, is this real?

192
00:06:18,096 --> 00:06:19,172
And I said,

193
00:06:19,172 --> 00:06:20,836
it's real, honey.

194
00:06:21,016 --> 00:06:22,467
It's pies.

195
00:06:22,647 --> 00:06:24,539
Six weeks of pies.

196
00:06:24,719 --> 00:06:26,844
And she picked up her phone,

197
00:06:26,844 --> 00:06:28,513
with her hand shaking,

198
00:06:28,513 --> 00:06:30,257
and called the manager.

199
00:06:30,437 --> 00:06:32,337
And I heard her whisper,

200
00:06:32,337 --> 00:06:34,315
I think I made a mistake.

201
00:06:34,666 --> 00:06:37,566
The manager came running down the escalator

202
00:06:37,566 --> 00:06:38,915
two steps at a time.

203
00:06:39,095 --> 00:06:41,015
A man in a gray suit,

204
00:06:41,015 --> 00:06:43,483
maybe fifty, out of breath.

205
00:06:43,663 --> 00:06:45,614
He'd talked to me on the phone

206
00:06:45,614 --> 00:06:46,264
that week.

207
00:06:46,444 --> 00:06:48,580
He said, Mrs.

208
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:49,550
Miller?

209
00:06:49,730 --> 00:06:52,168
The coat lady from Mansfield?

210
00:06:52,348 --> 00:06:54,461
I read about you in the paper.

211
00:06:54,641 --> 00:06:57,184
And he shook my hand with both

212
00:06:57,184 --> 00:06:58,540
of his and said,

213
00:06:58,540 --> 00:06:59,981
welcome, welcome.

214
00:07:00,161 --> 00:07:02,128
Right in front of that clerk,

215
00:07:02,128 --> 00:07:04,297
and the woman with the stroller,

216
00:07:04,297 --> 00:07:05,247
and everybody.

217
00:07:05,597 --> 00:07:07,840
So far, the windbreaker,

218
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,896
the pies, the counter,

219
00:07:09,896 --> 00:07:11,204
and the check.

220
00:07:11,384 --> 00:07:14,120
Then the manager turned to that clerk

221
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:15,377
and said, Ashley,

222
00:07:15,377 --> 00:07:17,003
you're done for today.

223
00:07:17,183 --> 00:07:18,878
Go to my office.

224
00:07:19,058 --> 00:07:20,851
And she started to cry,

225
00:07:20,851 --> 00:07:21,787
right there.

226
00:07:21,967 --> 00:07:25,117
And here's the part people don't expect,

227
00:07:25,117 --> 00:07:25,589
honey.

228
00:07:25,769 --> 00:07:27,557
I said, no.

229
00:07:27,737 --> 00:07:28,654
Please.

230
00:07:28,834 --> 00:07:31,133
I'd like Ashley to help me.

231
00:07:31,313 --> 00:07:33,640
I'd like her to pick out every

232
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,191
single coat with me.

233
00:07:35,371 --> 00:07:37,310
All hundred and forty.

234
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:40,170
The manager looked at me like I

235
00:07:40,170 --> 00:07:40,980
was crazy.

236
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:43,763
Ashley looked at me like I was

237
00:07:43,763 --> 00:07:44,283
crazy.

238
00:07:44,463 --> 00:07:45,397
But I said,

239
00:07:45,397 --> 00:07:47,861
Ashley, I want you to see who

240
00:07:47,861 --> 00:07:49,560
these coats are for.

241
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:51,570
And so for three hours,

242
00:07:51,570 --> 00:07:54,276
that girl and I went through every

243
00:07:54,276 --> 00:07:54,674
rack.

244
00:07:54,854 --> 00:07:56,779
I told her about the boy in

245
00:07:56,779 --> 00:07:57,919
the windbreaker.

246
00:07:58,099 --> 00:08:00,544
About the girl who wore her mother's

247
00:08:00,544 --> 00:08:02,174
coat down to her ankles.

248
00:08:02,354 --> 00:08:04,854
About the twins who shared one coat

249
00:08:04,854 --> 00:08:07,068
and took turns going to school.

250
00:08:07,418 --> 00:08:10,194
Somewhere around coat number sixty,

251
00:08:10,194 --> 00:08:13,606
honey, Ashley stopped talking on her phone.

252
00:08:13,786 --> 00:08:15,926
Around coat number ninety,

253
00:08:15,926 --> 00:08:18,314
she started asking questions.

254
00:08:18,494 --> 00:08:21,721
Is the little girl's favorite color purple?

255
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:24,391
Does the boy need a big size,

256
00:08:24,391 --> 00:08:26,452
so it lasts two winters?

257
00:08:26,632 --> 00:08:29,115
And around coat number one hundred and

258
00:08:29,115 --> 00:08:31,336
twenty, she told me she'd grown up

259
00:08:31,336 --> 00:08:33,296
in a trailer in southern Ohio,

260
00:08:33,296 --> 00:08:35,583
and her mother had bought her coats

261
00:08:35,583 --> 00:08:36,628
at the Goodwill.

262
00:08:36,979 --> 00:08:38,836
She said, Mrs.

263
00:08:39,016 --> 00:08:41,268
Miller, I'm so ashamed.

264
00:08:41,448 --> 00:08:43,656
When I saw your coat and your

265
00:08:43,656 --> 00:08:45,407
boots, I saw my mother.

266
00:08:45,587 --> 00:08:47,976
And I've spent five years trying to

267
00:08:47,976 --> 00:08:50,024
get as far away from my mother

268
00:08:50,024 --> 00:08:50,638
as I can.

269
00:08:50,818 --> 00:08:52,005
I got this job,

270
00:08:52,005 --> 00:08:53,270
and these nails,

271
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:54,694
and these clothes,

272
00:08:54,694 --> 00:08:57,147
so nobody would ever look at me

273
00:08:57,147 --> 00:08:59,520
the way people used to look at

274
00:08:59,520 --> 00:08:59,758
us.

275
00:08:59,938 --> 00:09:02,321
And then I looked at you exactly

276
00:09:02,321 --> 00:09:02,991
that way.

277
00:09:03,171 --> 00:09:04,796
I'm so sorry.

278
00:09:05,147 --> 00:09:06,821
I took her hands,

279
00:09:06,821 --> 00:09:08,594
red nails and all,

280
00:09:08,594 --> 00:09:09,677
and I said,

281
00:09:09,677 --> 00:09:11,056
honey, I know.

282
00:09:11,236 --> 00:09:13,565
I know that feeling better than you

283
00:09:13,565 --> 00:09:13,964
think.

284
00:09:14,144 --> 00:09:16,314
But the way out of being looked

285
00:09:16,314 --> 00:09:18,343
down on isn't to look down on

286
00:09:18,343 --> 00:09:19,322
somebody else.

287
00:09:19,502 --> 00:09:20,733
It's to look up.

288
00:09:20,913 --> 00:09:22,056
And she cried,

289
00:09:22,056 --> 00:09:23,035
and I cried,

290
00:09:23,035 --> 00:09:26,137
and the manager brought us both coffee

291
00:09:26,137 --> 00:09:28,667
and pretended he wasn't crying,

292
00:09:28,667 --> 00:09:28,994
too.

293
00:09:29,174 --> 00:09:31,368
We finished at five o'clock.

294
00:09:31,718 --> 00:09:34,864
The manager gave us twenty percent off.

295
00:09:35,044 --> 00:09:37,606
Then he took another hundred dollars out

296
00:09:37,606 --> 00:09:38,759
of his own wallet.

297
00:09:38,939 --> 00:09:41,031
And with the money we saved,

298
00:09:41,031 --> 00:09:43,123
we bought thirty more coats,

299
00:09:43,123 --> 00:09:43,571
honey.

300
00:09:43,751 --> 00:09:45,758
A hundred and seventy coats,

301
00:09:45,758 --> 00:09:48,051
in a moving truck the store paid

302
00:09:48,051 --> 00:09:51,133
for, delivered to Lincoln Elementary on the

303
00:09:51,133 --> 00:09:53,283
Monday before Christmas break.

304
00:09:53,634 --> 00:09:55,828
And then the part I promised you.

305
00:09:56,008 --> 00:09:58,040
The Sunday after Christmas,

306
00:09:58,040 --> 00:09:59,997
I was sitting in my pew at

307
00:09:59,997 --> 00:10:02,556
church, and the door opened at the

308
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:04,588
back, and in walked Ashley.

309
00:10:04,768 --> 00:10:06,405
No red nails.

310
00:10:06,585 --> 00:10:08,152
No designer coat.

311
00:10:08,332 --> 00:10:10,184
And with her was a little woman

312
00:10:10,184 --> 00:10:11,618
in a worn Goodwill coat.

313
00:10:11,798 --> 00:10:12,668
Her mother.

314
00:10:12,848 --> 00:10:16,111
They'd driven two hours from southern Ohio.

315
00:10:16,291 --> 00:10:18,688
Ashley walked her mother right down the

316
00:10:18,688 --> 00:10:20,470
aisle and sat her next to me.

317
00:10:20,821 --> 00:10:22,206
After the service,

318
00:10:22,206 --> 00:10:25,209
Ashley handed the minister an envelope.

319
00:10:25,389 --> 00:10:27,432
She'd been saving all month.

320
00:10:27,612 --> 00:10:29,145
Three hundred dollars,

321
00:10:29,145 --> 00:10:31,792
honey, from a girl making nine dollars

322
00:10:31,792 --> 00:10:32,349
an hour.

323
00:10:32,529 --> 00:10:34,236
For next year's coats.

324
00:10:34,416 --> 00:10:35,493
And she said,

325
00:10:35,493 --> 00:10:37,481
I want to help you bake.

326
00:10:37,661 --> 00:10:39,786
Every Saturday next fall.

327
00:10:39,966 --> 00:10:41,359
If you'll teach me.

328
00:10:41,539 --> 00:10:42,321
And I said,

329
00:10:42,321 --> 00:10:45,096
honey, I'll teach you my mother's fruit

330
00:10:45,096 --> 00:10:47,657
bread, and I've never taught that to

331
00:10:47,657 --> 00:10:48,226
anybody.

332
00:10:48,577 --> 00:10:51,526
Ashley's been baking with me every fall

333
00:10:51,526 --> 00:10:52,888
for sixteen years,

334
00:10:52,888 --> 00:10:53,720
sweetheart.

335
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:56,268
She runs the whole coat drive now.

336
00:10:56,448 --> 00:10:59,297
Last winter, we bought four hundred coats

337
00:10:59,297 --> 00:11:01,243
for three different schools.

338
00:11:01,423 --> 00:11:04,031
She's a manager at that same store,

339
00:11:04,031 --> 00:11:06,640
and every new clerk she trains gets

340
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:07,832
the same speech.

341
00:11:08,012 --> 00:11:10,090
She tells them about the old lady

342
00:11:10,090 --> 00:11:11,286
in the rain bonnet.

343
00:11:11,466 --> 00:11:12,807
And she tells them,

344
00:11:12,807 --> 00:11:15,418
you never know who's standing at your

345
00:11:15,418 --> 00:11:15,982
counter.

346
00:11:16,332 --> 00:11:18,799
Here's what I want you to remember,

347
00:11:18,799 --> 00:11:19,222
honey.

348
00:11:19,402 --> 00:11:21,859
When somebody treats you badly,

349
00:11:21,859 --> 00:11:23,207
you can walk out,

350
00:11:23,207 --> 00:11:25,347
or you can make them sorry.

351
00:11:25,527 --> 00:11:27,582
Or there's a third way.

352
00:11:27,762 --> 00:11:30,098
You can make them part of something

353
00:11:30,098 --> 00:11:30,432
good.

354
00:11:30,612 --> 00:11:32,481
It's the hardest of the three.

355
00:11:32,661 --> 00:11:35,103
But it's the only one that changes

356
00:11:35,103 --> 00:11:35,749
anything.

357
00:11:35,929 --> 00:11:38,074
And it's the one my mother would

358
00:11:38,074 --> 00:11:38,878
have chosen.

359
00:11:39,229 --> 00:11:41,155
Tell me in the comments,

360
00:11:41,155 --> 00:11:42,038
sweetheart.

361
00:11:42,218 --> 00:11:44,644
Would you have stayed and let Ashley

362
00:11:44,644 --> 00:11:47,001
help, or would you have walked out?

363
00:11:47,181 --> 00:11:49,039
Be honest with Grandma.

364
00:11:49,219 --> 00:11:51,639
There are more of my family's stories

365
00:11:51,639 --> 00:11:53,929
in the playlist right there on your

366
00:11:53,929 --> 00:11:55,956
screen, and I'll see you in the

367
00:11:55,956 --> 00:11:56,545
next one.

368
00:11:56,725 --> 00:11:58,408
Good night, honey.

369
00:11:58,588 --> 00:12:00,133
Merry Christmas.
